        {
          "url": "https://www.indiehackers.com/post/i-don-t-need-more-traffic-i-need-to-find-the-right-people-997e26cc20",
          "author_body": "Finding complaints wasn’t the problem. I went through about 20 recent threads across Reddit, Quora, LinkedIn, and a few freelancer forums. Plenty of people were frustrated with tools, prices, and subscriptions. But only 3–5 struck me as potentially open to switching. That’s my own judgment, not a measured sign of buying intent. Most seemed to be venting rather than actively looking for an alternative. I’m building LockMargin, a local-first financial OS for freelancers and small studios. It brings together time tracking, project management, invoicing, expenses, and profitability analytics. The idea is simple: show what you actually make after expenses, not just what you earn. And which projects are worth your time. To find my first users, I built a small tool that looks for people talking about invoicing problems. The plan seemed simple enough: find the conversations, join them where I could add something useful, and learn. I was also looking for the bigger problem behind my product: tracking time against projects, figuring out actual hourly rates, knowing which projects pay. I barely saw those topics in the threads I checked. I’m not sure what to make of that. Maybe freelancers discuss these problems elsewhere, or use different words for them. Maybe I was looking in the wrong places. Twenty threads is a pretty small sample. What I do know is that finding people unhappy with their current tools isn’t the same as finding people who want to change. And I still haven’t figured out how to reliably tell the difference. For now, I’d rather understand a few relevant conversations than collect hundreds of mentions that go nowhere. If you’ve looked for your first users this way, how do you tell a passing complaint from a real interest in switching?",
